File: perlthon.info,  Node: perlthon.use,  Next: perlthon.eval,  Up: API Reference

2.1 ‘perlthon.use(module_name)’
===============================

Load a Perl module, equivalent to Perl's ‘use’ statement.  Returns a
‘PerlModule’ proxy object where attribute access maps to function calls
in that module's namespace.

     json = perlthon.use("JSON::PP")

   The module is loaded idempotently -- calling ‘use()’ multiple times
for the same module is safe and will not raise an error.

   Raises ‘RuntimeError’ if the module cannot be found or loaded.


File: perlthon.info,  Node: perlthon.eval,  Next: perlthon.call,  Prev: perlthon.use,  Up: API Reference

2.2 ‘perlthon.eval(code)’
=========================

Evaluate a string of Perl code and return the result, automatically
converted to a Python type.

     perlthon.eval('[1, 2, 3]')         # [1, 2, 3]
     perlthon.eval('{"a" => 1}')      # {"a": 1}
     perlthon.eval('undef')             # None

   Raises ‘RuntimeError’ if the Perl code fails to compile or run.


File: perlthon.info,  Node: perlthon.call,  Next: PerlModule,  Prev: perlthon.eval,  Up: API Reference

2.3 ‘perlthon.call(function_name, *args)’
=========================================

Call a Perl function by its fully qualified name.

     perlthon.call("POSIX::floor", 3.7)  # 3.0

   Arguments are automatically converted from Python to Perl types
before being passed to the function.


File: perlthon.info,  Node: PerlModule,  Prev: perlthon.call,  Up: API Reference

2.4 PerlModule Proxy
====================

The ‘PerlModule’ object returned by ‘perlthon.use()’ supports attribute
access for calling functions:

     posix = perlthon.use("POSIX")
     posix.floor(3.7)  # calls POSIX::floor(3.7)

   For OO-style method dispatch (passing the module name as the
invocant), use the ‘call()’ method:

     module.call("method", arg1, arg2)


